---
import Layout from "../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import Navbar from "../../components/Navbar.astro";
import Footer from "../../components/Footer.astro";
---

<Layout title="Standardizing RFQ Workflows Across Divisions - Marker">
  <Navbar />
  <main id="main-content">
    <article class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-6 pt-16 pb-16 md:pt-24 md:pb-24">
      <a href="/case-studies" class="mb-8 inline-flex items-center text-sm text-muted-foreground hover:text-foreground">
        <svg class="mr-1 h-4 w-4" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2">
          <path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" d="M15 19l-7-7 7-7"></path>
        </svg>
        All Case Studies
      </a>

      <span class="mb-3 inline-block text-xs font-medium uppercase tracking-wider text-primary">Fortune 500 Retail</span>
      <h1 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-foreground md:text-3xl">Standardizing RFQ Workflows Across Divisions</h1>

      <div class="mt-8 rounded-lg border border-border bg-muted/50 p-5 text-sm">
        <div class="grid gap-4 sm:grid-cols-3">
          <div>
            <span class="block font-medium text-foreground">Company</span>
            <span class="text-muted-foreground">Fortune 500 mass-market retailer with US, international, and club store divisions</span>
          </div>
          <div>
            <span class="block font-medium text-foreground">Team</span>
            <span class="text-muted-foreground">Sourcing product insights team within a large enterprise sourcing organization</span>
          </div>
          <div>
            <span class="block font-medium text-foreground">Key Challenge</span>
            <span class="text-muted-foreground">RFQs running in multiple tools with no standardization across divisions, each with separate data structures</span>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <section class="mt-10">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold text-foreground">The Challenge</h2>
        <p class="mt-3 leading-relaxed text-muted-foreground">A product manager in the sourcing insights group at this Fortune 500 retailer described a fundamental fragmentation problem: the company's US operations, international business, and club store division each maintained completely separate databases and data structures. RFQs were run in multiple tools with no standardization. Associates had to navigate too many different systems to complete basic sourcing tasks.</p>
        <p class="mt-3 leading-relaxed text-muted-foreground">The data was "everywhere across different cloud services with no semantic layer," as the PM put it — "no one knows how everything is connected." The sourcing organization was also resistant to adopting iterative product solutions. Teams wanted full end-to-end capabilities or they would revert to spreadsheets. Without transparency into the logic behind any tool's recommendations, associates would continue defaulting to Excel.</p>
        <p class="mt-3 leading-relaxed text-muted-foreground">At the same time, the company was actively clamping down on point-solution AI tools proliferating across the organization. The challenge was clear: bring consistency to sourcing workflows across divisions without adding yet another disconnected tool to the stack.</p>
      </section>

      <section class="mt-10">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold text-foreground">The Approach</h2>
        <p class="mt-3 leading-relaxed text-muted-foreground">Marker sits on top of the existing communication and workflow layer rather than requiring data migration or system consolidation. By capturing the RFQ process as it happens — through email exchanges, document sharing, and supplier responses — Marker creates a standardized view of sourcing activity across divisions without requiring each division to adopt a single system of record.</p>
        <p class="mt-3 leading-relaxed text-muted-foreground">For associates, this means they can continue using the tools they know while Marker normalizes the data flowing between systems. When a sourcing associate in the club store division needs to compare terms with a vendor already used by the US team, that information is accessible through the communication record — not locked in a division-specific database. Every recommendation Marker surfaces is traceable back to the source communications, providing the transparency that the sourcing organization requires for adoption.</p>
      </section>

      <section class="mt-10">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold text-foreground">Key Takeaways</h2>
        <ul class="mt-3 space-y-2 text-muted-foreground">
          <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
            <span class="mt-1.5 block h-1.5 w-1.5 shrink-0 rounded-full bg-primary"></span>
            <span>Cross-division visibility into RFQ activity without requiring database consolidation or system migration</span>
          </li>
          <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
            <span class="mt-1.5 block h-1.5 w-1.5 shrink-0 rounded-full bg-primary"></span>
            <span>Associates keep existing tools — Marker normalizes data across workflows rather than replacing them</span>
          </li>
          <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
            <span class="mt-1.5 block h-1.5 w-1.5 shrink-0 rounded-full bg-primary"></span>
            <span>Full traceability from any recommendation back to the source communication, building trust with skeptical teams</span>
          </li>
          <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
            <span class="mt-1.5 block h-1.5 w-1.5 shrink-0 rounded-full bg-primary"></span>
            <span>Vendor terms and pricing visible across divisions, preventing duplicated negotiations</span>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section class="mt-12 rounded-lg border border-primary/20 bg-primary/5 p-6 text-center">
        <p class="text-sm font-medium text-foreground">See how Marker can unify sourcing workflows across your organization.</p>
        <a href="/book-demo" class="mt-4 inline-flex h-10 items-center justify-center rounded-lg bg-primary px-6 text-sm font-medium text-primary-foreground transition-colors hover:bg-primary/90 focus-visible:outline-none focus-visible:ring-2 focus-visible:ring-ring focus-visible:ring-offset-2">Book a Demo</a>
      </section>
    </article>
  </main>
  <Footer />
</Layout>
